# Source, provenance and bounded priority summary

Audit cutoff: 6 October 2026. Numeric upstream ID 5100032, code AMR-050-0032; original PR110 head `3d159f0a00d0bd7ff8d558d6fbc75af5094f7a35`. Original proof effort 2/5; this preparation adds no central proof-search turn. The dataset revision was `37e53eabe540fb458758e198be61634bd02ee008`. The nonempty inherited imported prior report was read and preserved, rather than treated as a current novelty certificate. `input_pins.json` identifies the exact original source/proof pair, prior report and controlling fresh root evidence by byte counts and SHA-256.

The literal target is equality of the two sums of ordinary positive Euclidean distances from the outer foci to the antipedal vertices of the original closed orbit polygon. Consecutive antipedal supporting lines pass through its original endpoints and are perpendicular to their focal displacement vectors. It concerns every regular closed orbit between strictly nested confocal ellipses, 0 < lambda < b²: every admissible period and winding, stars, reversals and repeats, including odd primitive periods. It asserts neither individual-sum constancy nor hyperbolic/degenerate extensions.

The observation is earlier work of Dan Reznik, Ronaldo Garcia and Jair Koiller. The actual source is *Eighty New Invariants of N-Periodics in the Elliptic Billiard*, arXiv:2004.12497v11 (29 October 2020), §§3.5/3.7 and Table 7; the published companion is *Fifty New Invariants of N-Periodics in the Elliptic Billiard*, Arnold Mathematical Journal 7 (2021), 341–355, DOI 10.1007/s40598-021-00174-y, §3.7 and Table 7 p.349. Its k603 row records value 1 for all N, an experimental discovery in May 2020 and an unknown-proof marker at that publication date. The marker is historical, not a guarantee of current openness.

The manuscript independently derives the needed ingredients. These are credited as background: classical negative-pedal/antipedal and inverse-polarity constructions (Salmon, *A Treatise on the Higher Plane Curves*, 3rd ed., 1879, Arts.121–122); the focal sideline-height product (Bialy–Tabachnikov, *Dan Reznik's identities and more*, DOI 10.1007/s40879-020-00428-7, Lemma 4.2); centrally paired cancellation and the ordinary closure telescope. The candidate proof contribution relative to the read prior corpus is the precise ordinary-positive norm difference `q_plus-q_minus=Gamma delta_y`, with fixed-confocal Gamma after selecting left orientation, and the resulting full-period equality. Earlier focus-inverse perimeter, centroid/vector, signed-area and primed-polygon statements were compared as different observables; no valid full-target transfer was found.

Three materially distinct fresh priority families examined exact invariant history/current follow-ups, classical confocal/negative-pedal mechanisms, and modern support/inversion/projective/averaging mechanisms. A fresh cross-family adjudication challenged the closest mappings, including the ordinary norm versus inverse-edge-length distinction and the need to cover odd primitive periods. Their original hold files are immutable and still report their then-pending journal-final comparison. They are historical evidence, not the current disposition.

That selected gap is now resolved by the complete legitimate eleven-page journal final: R. Garcia, J. Koiller and D. Reznik, *Estimating Elliptic Billiard Invariants with Spatial Integrals*, Journal of Dynamical and Control Systems 29 (2023), 757–767, online 10 August 2022, DOI 10.1007/s10883-022-09608-y. The supplied final PDF pin is 1,150,664 bytes, SHA-256 `8df802b8b6255aae648934fd83aab4079a5d6b4c66382a9b1b26a6cd3237eac3`. Root and a separate adversarial reviewer each read the complete final and the complete nine-page precursor, *Average Elliptic Billiard Invariants with Spatial Integrals*, arXiv:2102.10899v1, 22 February 2021. Its current final includes actual version changes and no antipedal target definition, covering result, citation asserting its proof, or valid transfer of its spatial averages to every finite rational-period norm